# The owner's review of the setup page and a last review's fixes, for the page's next image (1.1.0, not published). On
# an Apple Silicon Mac, Docker Desktop, no umbrelOS, no cost. The page from this checkout; relay 0.3.0's image and the
# package's other images as pinned. The mocked suites, the browser test, the previews and the screenshots on ports 8851
# to 8858 only (security.py and cloudflare.py each bind a port the system picks, as before), then check.py on a fresh
# up.sh run with the page on 8859, removed afterwards with down.sh, then Umbrel's linter.
# Times are UTC: on this Mac it was 2026-11-17, 21:29 PST.

# Earlier the same day, from the owner's review, committed before this run and tested here on the package's containers
# for the first time:
# - Each address option says who it's for, in place of one Recommended badge, and what happens after it is folded away
#   under What happens next. Under the address boxes, the first part is up to the owner, and once a domain is typed, the
#   full address.
# - The public options say members join from anywhere with an invite.
# - The cert.pem note's sentence to delete the file is in bold.
# - The install link goes to buzz.xyz, Block's page for every platform, not the GitHub release page.
# - The footer links the relay's repo and the app's.
# - The Moderation row's grey panels became four short notes in the guide's style, a one-sentence To open it, and Buzz's
#   three Unavailable reasons folded into Moderation doesn't show in Buzz?.
# - The agents caption in the owner's words, and the Builderlab fold as Buzz 1.0.5 and the phone app have it.

# The last review's fixes, by its items, with the texts before and after:
# 1. The Moderation doesn't show in Buzz? fold starts with the page's own check, and the third message's note ends at the
#    clock. Buzz shows Could not check access for an HTML page or a 5xx too, not only for a network that blocks the port
#    (toAccess in src/bundled/relay-staff/staff.ts at block/buzz-app v1.0.5: an intercepted answer, and an ambiguous one
#    such as a 5xx, is "unreachable"), and the third message for any 401 or 403 that isn't an HTML page. So a problem
#    the page shows can be behind either.
#    "In Buzz, open Settings, then Plugins, Manage plugins. If Moderation says Unavailable, point at its button to see
#    why:" -> "If this page shows a moderation problem, fix that first. In Buzz, open Settings, then Plugins, Manage
#    plugins. If Moderation says Unavailable, point at its button to see why:"
#    "... Also check that your computer sets its clock automatically. If this page shows a moderation problem, fix that
#    first." -> "... Also check that your computer sets its clock automatically."
# 2. The fold inside the Moderation row took the Settings rows' rules: a full-width summary with the rows' padding, its
#    arrow after the words, and the rows' padding around its body. The rows' rules now reach only the card's own folds,
#    its children (.set-card > details), so the fold inside is a small fold like the page's others: an inline summary
#    with its arrow first, none after, and its body indented 14 pixels.
# 3. The private network's label, on the setup card and in Settings, Address:
#    "Recommended when the server must stay off the internet" -> "Recommended when the server must not be reachable from
#    the internet". Step 2's intro to match: "... or a private group behind a VPN that only certain people can reach."
#    -> "... or a private group whose server must not be reachable from the internet."
# 4. Choosing your own domain made the setup page 561 pixels wide at 375: the options' grid had a column without a size,
#    which grew to the domain card's widest content. Now .choices { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }.
# 5. The footer: "Buzz is open source, from Block. The relay is at github.com/block/buzz, and the app at
#    github.com/block/buzz-app." -> "Buzz is open source, from Block. The relay is at github.com/block/buzz, the desktop
#    app at github.com/block/buzz-app, and the phone app at github.com/block/buzz-mobile." Each a link, target="_blank"
#    rel="noopener". The phone app moved to block/buzz-mobile on 2026-11-17.
# 6. The Builderlab fold says where to sign out, in a paragraph of its own:
#    "A phone that already has a community doesn't offer email sign-in: sign out of Builderlab on your computer to get
#    the code instead." -> "A phone that already has a community doesn't offer email sign-in. To get the code instead,
#    sign out of Builderlab on your computer: open Settings, then Identity, and choose Sign out on your Builderlab
#    account, app.builderlab.xyz. Your Buzz identity stays on the computer. Don't use Remove from device: it's a
#    different button, and it removes your key from the computer."
#    "Buzz also saves this community's address to your Builderlab account." now ends the first paragraph.
#    From block/buzz-app at v1.0.5 (a local clone, read-only git): in a Builderlab build that sign-in is managed in
#    Settings, Identity, where the Builderlab card's Manage account leads (src/bundled/builderlab/login/Login.tsx). Under
#    Community sign-ins, the account's card is named by its host, app.builderlab.xyz, since the built-in server has no
#    organization name (src/features/federated-identity/built-in-server.json, serverTitle in src/app/IdentitySettings.tsx).
#    Its button reads Sign out; then a dialog, Sign out of app.builderlab.xyz?, says "Your Buzz identity stays on this
#    device" and has Sign out again. Signed out, Settings, Mobile app shows the pairing code (MobileApp.tsx). Remove from
#    device, under the Buzz ID card, removes the private key from this device's secure storage
#    (src/features/identity/SignOut.tsx).
# 7. The agents caption's link names where it goes: "<a ...>The self-hosting guide</a> shows how to run an agent on its
#    own server." -> "The self-hosting guide shows <a ...>how to run an agent on its own server</a>." The same href,
#    https://self-host.buzz/#agents, target and rel.
# 8. Moderation's address is on the internet: "https://buzz.yourteam.com:8443, open only to requests you or your staff
#    sign." -> "https://buzz.yourteam.com:8443, on the internet, open only to requests you or your staff sign."
# And Back after Moderation's link to Keep it safe. The script handled that link as the guide's own links, the address
# replaced, so Back left the page. Now the link adds one entry, its state { buzzStep }, and the entry it leaves keeps the
# link, { buzzFrom }. Back to that entry fires popstate: the page opens the link's fold, moves focus to the link, scrolls
# it into view if it isn't, and leaves the guide as it is. hashchange skips those entries, so Back opens no other step,
# and Forward opens the step again. After popstate, Chrome goes to the element the address names (#phone here) and takes
# focus from anything outside it, so the page moves focus in the next task: moved during popstate, it fell to the page.
# The guide's own links and Next still replace the address, and a step typed into the address bar still opens it.
